## The holiday you come home exhausted from

You know the feeling. A week off, and yet you return drained. Your body rested; your mind did not. The reason usually sits in your pocket: the phone that kept you tethered to work, messages and an endless stream of news throughout your supposed "holiday". Truly switching off no longer begins when you leave home — it begins the moment you stop reacting to every ping.

This is where a spa town can offer something an ordinary beach holiday rarely manages: a setting and a daily rhythm that draw you away from the screen quite naturally. A **digital detox in Mariánské Lázně** is not a wellness buzzword stuck on a brochure. It is a return to what the spa cure has always been about — slowing down, regularity, and attention paid to your own body.

## What happens when you switch off

That constant connectivity does us no good is no longer merely a hunch. Research from recent years offers concrete pointers — and they are worth knowing before you decide to "unplug" for a week.

**Sleep** plays a key role. The blue light of screens in the evening suppresses the release of melatonin, the hormone that signals to the body that it is time to sleep. The result is later sleep onset and shorter, less restorative sleep. Review articles and smaller controlled trials alike agree that less screen time before bed makes falling asleep easier — in one experiment, simply blocking short-wavelength light in the evening shortened sleep onset by roughly seven minutes. The reverse holds too: people who spend less time on their phones during the day sleep measurably better.

The second piece of the puzzle is **nature**. Time spent in woodland and greenery measurably lowers levels of the stress hormone cortisol and reduces perceived stress. In one study, the proportion of participants in the "low stress" band rose from under a fifth to more than half after a series of forest walks. Mariánské Lázně, surrounded by the Slavkovský les (Slavkov Forest) protected landscape area, therefore offers exactly the combination the research recommends: less screen, more forest.

This is not a miracle cure, nor about swapping one dependency for another. It is a simple principle — giving the body and nervous system an environment that does not keep them on alert but genuinely calms them.

## A landscape made for slowing down

Mariánské Lázně brings a natural advantage to a digital detox, one it does not have to manufacture. The town sits at **around 630 metres above sea level**, ringed by forest, and since 2023 has held the status of a climatic spa. The mild sub-montane climate is itself toning — both invigorating and calming.

The spa centre is also laid out to lead you outdoors. The colonnade, the parks, the promenades and more than a hundred cold mineral springs in the surrounding area form a network of paths walked slowly, cup in hand. The forest trails of the Slavkov Forest often lead precisely to springs hidden among the trees — here, walking has a destination and a purpose, and you reach for your phone only for a photograph.

That is the crucial difference from a city break, where every pause tempts you to glance at the screen. Here the pause is filled by a spring, a bench with a view, or simply the silence of the forest.

## A week without notifications: what it looks like

What might such a spa detox look like in practice? The figures below are illustrative — take them not as a target but as a picture of what can replace the time once spent at a screen during a classic week-long stay:

- **7 days without notifications** — the phone on "do not disturb", email off, a single set window for messages once a day
- **42 km of walking** — roughly six kilometres a day along the colonnade, parks and forest trails
- **21 cups at the spring** — the drinking cure spread across the day, three cups a day as advised by a doctor

The point lies not in perfect numbers but in replacing a habit. Where mindless scrolling once came, a cup at the [Cross Spring](/en/mineral-springs), a walk or a treatment now takes its place. The brain gets the regularity and predictability it craves — just not from a screen.

## Rituals that replace the scroll

The spa day has a rhythm that needs no phone. You only have to follow it.

**The morning drinking cure.** The walk to the spring early in the morning, on an empty stomach, is in itself a small meditation in motion. The [drinking cure](/en/mineral-springs) from the Mariánské Lázně springs has its rules — pace, breathing, calm — and all are the opposite of the hurried morning scroll through the news.

**Walking as the programme, not as a transfer.** Mariánské Lázně offers some of the [finest walks](/en/magazine/best-walks) in Bohemia, as well as deeper forest trails. Nordic walking, a slow walk through the woods, or simply sitting by the Forest Spring — all of it works better when the route is set not by a satnav but by your own pace.

**Treatments as time for yourself.** A carbon-dioxide bath, a peat wrap or a massage are hours during which you would not pick up your phone anyway. Treat them as the natural anchors of the day, around which the rest of your "offline" time arranges itself easily.

**The evening without a screen.** It is the evening that decides the quality of your sleep. A book, a spa concert or a stroll at dusk will do more for your recovery than one last check of your email.

## Practical tips for making it work

Letting go at the first attempt is not always easy. A few small things make it considerably simpler:

- **Set an out-of-office reply** before you leave. It grants colleagues — and yourself — permission not to respond at once.
- **Switch off notifications, not the whole phone.** The goal is not unreachability at any cost, but an end to interruptions. Reserve one short window each day for anything genuinely urgent.
- **Replace your alarm clock.** A traditional alarm by the bed means the phone need not be in the bedroom — and you will not reach for it first thing.
- **Have a plan for boredom.** The first two days are usually the hardest. Keep a book, a trail map or a list of springs you want to discover close to hand.
- **Expect an adjustment period.** The restlessness of the first days is normal. It usually fades once the body settles into the spa rhythm.

A digital detox in Mariánské Lázně is not about denial. It is an exchange — you trade noise for silence, scrolling for the spring, the blue light of a screen for the golden light over the forest. And it is precisely from a holiday like this that you return not only rested, but with the sense that you finally had time to be alone with yourself. If you are planning a longer stay, look too at the [accommodation](/en/accommodation) options within the spa zone, from where every spring is just a few steps away.
